„¥verse#¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ©Say not ye, There are yet four months, and then cometh harvest? behold, I say unto you, Lift up your eyes, and look on the fields; for they are white already to harvest.¦layout ªlast_verse#„¥verse$¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙAnd he that reapeth receiveth wages, and gathereth fruit unto life eternal: that both he that soweth and he that reapeth may rejoice together.¦layout ªlast_verse$„¥verse%¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ@And herein is that saying true, One soweth, and another reapeth.¦layout ªlast_verse%„¥verse&¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙqI sent you to reap that whereon ye bestowed no labour: other men laboured, and ye are entered into their labours.¦layout ªlast_verse&…¤book*§chapter«first_verse'ªlast_verse*¦verses”„¥verse'¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ…And many of the Samaritans of that city believed on him for the saying of the woman, which testified, He told me all that ever I did.¦layout ªlast_verse'„¥verse(¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙxSo when the Samaritans were come unto him, they besought him that he would tarry with them: and he abode there two days.¦layout ªlast_verse(„¥verse)¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ/And many more believed because of his own word;¦layout ªlast_verse)„¥verse*¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ§and said unto the woman, Now we believe, not because of thy saying: for we have heard him ourselves, and know that this is indeed the Christ, the Saviour of the world.¦layout ªlast_verse*…¤book*§chapter«first_verse+ªlast_verse-¦verses“„¥verse+¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ=Now after two days he departed thence, and went into Galilee.¦layout ªlast_verse+„¥verse,¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙNFor Jesus himself testified, that a prophet hath no honour in his own country.¦layout ªlast_verse,„¥verse-¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¥Then when he was come into Galilee, the GalilÃ¦ans received him, having seen all the things that he did at Jerusalem at the feast: for they also went unto the feast.¦layout ªlast_verse-…¤book*§chapter«first_verse.ªlast_verse6¦verses™„¥verse.¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙŠSo Jesus came again into Cana of Galilee, where he made the water wine. And there was a certain nobleman, whose son was sick at Capernaum.¦layout ªlast_verse.„¥verse/¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ­When he heard that Jesus was come out of JudÃ¦a into Galilee, he went unto him, and besought him that he would come down, and heal his son: for he was at the point of death.¦layout ªlast_verse/„¥verse0¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙOThen said Jesus unto him, Except ye see signs and wonders, ye will not believe.¦layout ªlast_verse0„¥verse1¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ=The nobleman saith unto him, Sir, come down ere my child die.¦layout ªlast_verse1„¥verse2¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ„Jesus saith unto him, Go thy way; thy son liveth. And the man believed the word that Jesus had spoken unto him, and he went his way.¦layout ªlast_verse2„¥verse3¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙYAnd as he was now going down, his servants met him, and told him, saying, Thy son liveth.¦layout ªlast_verse3„¥verse4¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙƒThen enquired he of them the hour when he began to amend. And they said unto him, Yesterday at the seventh hour the fever left him.¦layout ªlast_verse4„¥verse5¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙSo the father knew that it was at the same hour, in the which Jesus said unto him, Thy son liveth: and himself believed, and his whole house.¦layout ªlast_verse5„¥verse6¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ]This is again the second miracle that Jesus did, when he was come out of JudÃ¦a into Galilee.¦layout ªlast_verse6